Cell["Relation to bundle terminology", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"d3e9d21b-9f45-4355-8dfc-df56be75dcbd"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The graph-bundle condition is edgewise: the preimage of each base edge is \
its Cartesian product with the common fiber, respecting the projection. This \
is the Cartesian graph-bundle convention in the graph literature; it permits \
global twisting. See ",
 ButtonBox["Algorithm for recognizing Cartesian graph bundles",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0166218X01002967"]\
, None}],
 "."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"d0b33f8a-4be5-43ce-b5ce-a1e7282ddae2"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A topological fiber bundle instead requires product charts over open \
neighbourhoods. Topological covering spaces have unique path lifting; a \
covering over a disconnected base can have different sheet counts. These are \
separate statements about topological spaces, as in ",
 ButtonBox["Hatcher, Algebraic Topology, Sections 1.3 and 4.2",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/AT.pdf"], None}],
 ". The finite predicates here assert neither Serre nor Hurewicz homotopy \
lifting."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"0f47f8d4-9758-4d63-9742-9dc3b1b321c5"],

Cell["\<\
The broad object and separate predicates follow the April 2026 Meeting 1 \
notes of Pavel Hajek, Ioana Milea and Stephen Wolfram, unpublished. Ioana \
Milea's one-page Fibered Graph Gauge Theory Basics motivates fiber \
automorphisms and permutation holonomy. A matching of sets becomes a \
fiber-graph isomorphism only when it also preserves vertical adjacency.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"0d6dee3d-a0fa-453e-9e22-b5eab34c78d6"],

